\cfg{man-identity}{cputty}{1}{2026-11-08}{PuTTY tool suite}{PuTTY tool suite}

\H{cputty-manpage} Man page for cputty

\S{cputty-manpage-name} NAME

\cw{cputty} \- cluster SSH client for X: one console typing into many PuTTY sessions

\S{cputty-manpage-synopsis} SYNOPSIS

\c cputty [ options ] [ host | group ... ]
\e bbbbbb   iiiiiii     iiii   iiiii

\S{cputty-manpage-description} DESCRIPTION

\cw{cputty} is a variant of \cw{putty}(\e{1}) in the style of the
classic \cw{cssh} cluster administration tools. It opens one PuTTY
terminal window for each \e{host} given on the command line, together
with a small \e{console} window.

Keystrokes typed into the console window are sent to every session
whose box is ticked in the console's host list. The \q{All}, \q{None}
and \q{Invert} buttons change which sessions are ticked. Keystrokes
typed into an individual terminal window go to that session only, so
you can switch freely between typing to the whole cluster and
attending to a single machine.

Each keystroke from the console is translated by each session
separately, so terminal modes such as application cursor keys are
honoured per session. Sessions whose connection has closed no longer
receive input from the console; close their windows directly.

The terminal windows open in a cascade from the top left of the
screen, each a little below and to the right of the last, and the
console is placed in the bottom right corner, in front of them. The
console's \q{Arrange windows} button tiles all the terminal windows
and the console evenly over the screen, in rows, with the console in
the bottom right corner. The terminals resize to fit their tiles; the
console is never made smaller than it can usefully be.

The console lists your host groups (see HOST GROUPS below).
Its \q{New session} button opens the configuration box for a new
session. Sessions started from a terminal window's menu (\q{New
Session}, \q{Duplicate Session} and \q{Saved Sessions}) also open in
the same process and join the console's host list.

The console stays open when all the sessions have closed, so that more
can be opened from it. Closing the console window closes all the
sessions and exits \cw{cputty}.

\S{cputty-manpage-groups} HOST GROUPS

A host group is a name for a set of hosts. The console shows a button
for each group: click it to open a session to each of the group's
hosts. To make a group, open and tick the hosts you want in it, press
\q{Save ticked hosts as group} and type a name. Right-click a group's button to
delete the group. A group name can also be given on the command line
in place of a host.

Groups are kept in a plain text file (see FILES below),
which can also be edited by hand. Each line is a group name followed
by its hosts, separated by spaces or tabs. Blank lines and lines
starting with \cw{#} are ignored, as is anything after a word starting
with \cw{#}. For example:

\c # name  hosts
\c web     arrange=yes web1 web2 root@web3:2222
\c db      db1 db2

Each host in a group is written the same way as a \e{host} argument on
the command line (see OPTIONS below). Words starting with
\cw{-} are ignored, so a group can't contain command-line options. The
console reads the file again whenever you switch to it.

A word containing \cw{=} is a setting for the group rather than a
host. \cw{arrange=yes} makes opening the group also arrange all the
windows, as the \q{Arrange windows} button does (\cw{arrange=no}, the
default, doesn't). The \q{Save ticked hosts as group} dialog has a
checkbox for it. Settings that \cw{cputty} doesn't recognise are
ignored.

\S{cputty-manpage-options} OPTIONS

\cw{cputty} accepts the same options as \cw{putty}(\e{1}). Each
option applies to every host, including hosts opened later from a
group.

Unlike \cw{putty}, \cw{cputty} treats \e{every} non-option argument as
a host, or as the name of a host group if there is a group of that
name. Each \e{host} is interpreted as \cw{putty} would interpret its
own host argument, so for example \cw{user@host} and the name of a
saved session are accepted. A host may also be followed by
\cw{:}\e{port} to connect to a non-default port (for an IPv6 address,
put it in square brackets first, as in \cw{[2001:db8::1]:2222}), or the
port for every host can be set with \cw{\-P} \e{port}.

If no \e{host} is given, \cw{cputty} opens just the console, from
which a group or a new session can be opened. If a saved session is
given with \cw{\-load}, that session is opened as well. On Windows,
if nothing is opened, the console asks for hosts to connect to; its
\q{Add hosts} button asks again at any time.

\S{cputty-manpage-files} FILES

\dt \cw{~/.config/putty/cputty-groups}

\dd The host groups file. It is kept in the same directory as the rest
of the PuTTY configuration, so it may instead be
\cw{~/.putty/cputty-groups}, or under \cw{$PUTTYDIR} if that is set.

\S{cputty-manpage-examples} EXAMPLES

\c cputty -l root web1 web2 web3

\c cputty -ssh -i ~/.ssh/admin.ppk admin@db1 admin@db2:2222

\c cputty -l root web

\S{cputty-manpage-limitations} LIMITATIONS

A size given with \cw{\-geometry} applies to every window. A position
given with \cw{\-geometry} is used for the first window, and the
others cascade from it.

Window positions can't be chosen on a Wayland display, so there the
desktop decides where the windows go.

A host whose name contains spaces or an \cw{=} sign (such as a saved
session with one in its name) can't be put in a group.

A password given with \cw{\-pw} is offered to every host.

\S{cputty-manpage-more-information} MORE INFORMATION

cputty's home page, with its releases and issue tracker, is
\W{https://github.com/gluffis/cputty}\cw{https://github.com/gluffis/cputty}.
cputty is a modified version of PuTTY and is not supported by the
PuTTY developers, so please report problems with it there.

\S{cputty-manpage-see-also} SEE ALSO

\cw{putty}(\e{1})
